{"id":"wikilinks","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a0","title":"[[Wikilinks]] in documents resolve to links — today they render as literal text","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 on the human's word (separate item, not folded into daily-notes): their vault's notes lean on Obsidian-style [[target]] and [[target|label]], which olai's markdown pipeline currently renders as literal bracket text (visible in the human's daily-note screenshot). Scope: resolve wikilinks at render time in the sanitising pipeline (markdown/render.ts + rewrite.ts territory) — [[target|label]] shows label, [[target]] shows target; resolution order: a served document whose basename or path matches → /doc/<path>; a node id match → /n/<id>; unresolved stays visibly distinct (muted, title says unresolved — never silently a dead link, per HACKING error-surfacing). Inline-only contexts (node titles via title.ts) get the same treatment. Tests over the resolution table and the sanitiser (a wikilink must not open an injection seam). Depends on nothing in flight; touches markdown/ only. NOT DISPATCHED."}
{"id":"5kz73j46","ord":"a1","title":"Agents"}
{"id":"47ps3bzc","parent":"5kz73j46","ord":"a0","title":"Orchestrate from the chat, not a terminal #human"}
{"id":"oic-wake","parent":"47ps3bzc","ord":"a0","title":"Nothing can wake a chat agent — the babysitting regression","todo":true,"desc":"Nothing can wake a chat agent — the babysitting regression. ACP turns are human-initiated; no MCP notification wakes an idle session; the MCP blocking waits (wait_agentState / wait_outputSettled) hold a turn open into the host's timeout.\n\nANSWERED IN DESIGN 2026-08-15 (the human asked \"can kolu MCP do this?\" — yes, with the right subscriber): the subscriber does not have to be the agent. Olai's server is the ACP CLIENT — the party that starts turns — and kolu MCP exposes subscribable snapshot-bearing resources: `urgency` (who needs attention) and `terminals` (per-terminal agent state), per kolu-mcp/src/expose.ts. So the wake path is kolu → olai's own subscription → olai starts a chat turn saying which lane settled. That build is the olai-subscribes node; this node closes when it lands. Known sharp edge for the first probe: kolu's `activity` resource cannot honor the read contract (fresh-subscriber-starts-empty, recorded in expose.ts:80) — urgency/terminals are the cells to lean on. Until then, babysitting stays the terminal orchestrator's detached watchers."}
{"id":"oic-shell","parent":"47ps3bzc","ord":"a1","title":"\"No shell, so no gh\" — contradicted; one live probe decides","todo":true,"desc":"FILED as \"No shell, so no gh\" — and CONTRADICTED 2026-08-15 by the human and by the code: the claim counted only the MCP servers the session is handed (olai's ops, kolu's) and forgot the agent's own built-ins. The chat runs the Claude Code adapter in bypassPermissions mode (interpret.ts:43, the 2026-08-09 design), and nothing puts Bash in disallowedTools (only AskUserQuestion gets that, and olai avoids it via elicitation.form) — so the built-in shell, and gh with it, should run without asking. ONE LIVE PROBE settles it: in a real chat session, run `gh pr checks` and confirm the tool call executes rather than stalls on a permission nobody can answer. If it runs, this node closes as wrong and the chat orchestrator's gh story is already whole; the original desc's real observations that survive either way — fs_readFile being repo-scoped, TUI screens truncating long reviews — matter only if the probe fails."}
